    " Whether `r` is `Ok`, without consuming it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::core\u{1f}Result::is_err\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Result::is_err<T"
    ", E>(ref<Result<T, E>, shared> r) : bool\u{1f}Whether `r` is `Err`, without consuming it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Resu"
    "lt::is_err<T, E>(ref<Result<T, E>, shared> r) : bool\n    in `std::core`\n\n    Whether `r` is `Err`, w"
    "ithout consuming it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::core\u{1f}swap\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn swap<T>(ref<T, exclusive> a, ref<T, exclusive> b)"
    "\u{1f}Exchanges the values behind `a` and `b`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn swap<T>(ref<T, exclusive> a, ref<T, exclusive> "
    "b)\n    in `std::core`\n\n    Exchanges the values behind `a` and `b`.\n\n    A program cannot move a val"
    "ue out of a place it reaches through a reference, so it cannot exchange two\n    fields of a borrowed"
    " struct itself; this does. Nothing is copied or destroyed: a resource is now owned by\n    the place "
    "it is in. Each place is written as `*a = v` would write it: a live reference that conflicts with\n   "
    " either one, such as a shared reborrow of `*a`, makes the exchange fault. For two elements of one `V"
    "ec` or\n    slice, use `Vec::swap` or `slice_swap`, which check the bounds and allow `i == j`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std:"
    ":core\u{1f}replace\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn replace<T>(ref<T, exclusive> r, T v) : T\u{1f}Puts `v` in `*r` and returns wha"
    "t was there.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn replace<T>(ref<T, exclusive> r, T v) : T\n    in `std::core`\n\n    Puts `v` in"
    " `*r` and returns what was there.\n\n    `swap` with a fresh value. `overwrite` destroys the old value"
    " instead of returning it.\n\n    Example, \"replace: the old value comes back\":\n        i32 a = 1;\n    "
    "    i32 old = replace(&mut a, 2);\n        assert(old == 1 && a == 2);\n        i32 b = 3;\n        swa"
    "p(&mut a, &mut b);\n        assert(a == 3 && b == 2);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::core\u{1f}overwrite\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn overwrite<T>"
    "(ref<T, exclusive> r, T v)\u{1f}Puts `v` in `*r` and destroys what was there, at the call.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn ove"
    "rwrite<T>(ref<T, exclusive> r, T v)\n    in `std::core`\n\n    Puts `v` in `*r` and destroys what was t"
    "here, at the call.\n\n    The way to assign over a place that holds a resource, which plain assignment"
    " refuses.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Vec<T>\u{1f}A growable array of `T`, ownin"
    "g its elements.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Vec<T>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    A growable array of `T"
    "`, owning its elements.\n\n    `v[i]` reads or writes element `i` (`index_out_of_bounds` past the end)"
    ", `&v[lo..hi]` is a slice of it,\n    and `foreach` visits it by value, `&v` or `&mut v`. A reference"
    " to an element is stale after a `push`\n    that reallocates. Dropping a `Vec` destroys its elements,"
    " first to last, then frees its buffer.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::new<T>() : Vec<"
    "T>\u{1f}A new, empty `Vec`; nothing is allocated until it first grows.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::new<T>() : Vec<T>\n"
    "    in `std::collections`\n\n    A new, empty `Vec`; nothing is allocated until it first grows.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std:"
    ":collections\u{1f}Vec::len\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::len<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v) : usize\u{1f}The number of elements"
    " in `v`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::len<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v) : usize\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    The nu"
    "mber of elements in `v`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::push\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::push<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusiv"
    "e> v, T x)\u{1f}Appends `x` to the end of `v`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::push<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, T x)\n   "
    " in `std::collections`\n\n    Appends `x` to the end of `v`.\n\n    Amortised constant time: when the bu"
    "ffer is full it is reallocated at twice its capacity, and references\n    to elements held across tha"
    "t are stale at their next use. Faults (`alloc_failure`) when memory runs out.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec"
    "::pop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::pop<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v) : Option<T>\u{1f}Removes the last element of `v`"
    " and returns it; `None` when `v` is empty.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::pop<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v) : Option"
    "<T>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Removes the last element of `v` and returns it; `None` when `v` i"
    "s empty.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::index_shared\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::index_shared<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> "
    "v, usize i) : ref<T, shared>\u{1f}The element at index `i` of `v`, as a shared reference; what `v[i]` rea"
    "ds, so programs write `v[i]`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::index_shared<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, usize i) : ref<"
    "T, shared>\n    in `std::collections`\n    requires\n        i < v.len, index_out_of_bounds\n\n    The el"
    "ement at index `i` of `v`, as a shared reference; what `v[i]` reads, so programs write `v[i]`.\n\n    "
    "Faults (`index_out_of_bounds`) when `i >= len`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::index_exclusive\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn"
    " Vec::index_exclusive<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i) : ref<T, exclusive>\u{1f}The element at index"
    " `i` of `v`, as an exclusive reference; what `v[i]` is when it is written, so programs write `v[i]`."
    "\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::index_exclusive<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize i) : ref<T, exclusive>\n    in `s"
    "td::collections`\n    requires\n        i < v.len, index_out_of_bounds\n\n    The element at index `i` o"
    "f `v`, as an exclusive reference; what `v[i]` is when it is written, so\n    programs write `v[i]`.\n\n"
    "    Faults (`index_out_of_bounds`) when `i >= len`. A live slice of `v` holding `i` conflicts with i"
    "t.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::reserve\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::reserve<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize n)\u{1f}M"
    "akes room for `n` more elements: afterwards the capacity is at least `len + n`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::rese"
    "rve<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, usize n)\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Makes room for `n` more ele"
    "ments: afterwards the capacity is at least `len + n`.\n\n    Grows at most once, to the larger of `len"
    " + n` and twice the capacity, so small reserves in a loop stay\n    amortised; never shrinks and neve"
    "r changes the length or the elements. It changes only how fast a\n    program runs. `len + n` overflo"
    "wing is `diag.arith-overflow`, before anything is allocated.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export "
    "fn Vec::drop<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Destroys every element of `self`, first to last, then f"
    "rees the buffer; the destructor run when a `Vec` goes out of scope, not called by programs.\u{1f}\u{1f}export "
    "fn Vec::drop<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> self)\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Destroys every element o"
    "f `self`, first to last, then frees the buffer; the destructor run when a `Vec`\n    goes out of scop"
    "e, not called by programs.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::sort_by\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::sort_by<T>(ref<Vec<T>, "
    "exclusive> v, fn(ref<T, shared>, ref<T, shared>) : bool less)\u{1f}Sorts `v` by `less`, which is true whe"
    "n its first argument goes before its second.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::sort_by<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v, fn"
    "(ref<T, shared>, ref<T, shared>) : bool less)\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Sorts `v` by `less`, wh"
    "ich is true when its first argument goes before its second.\n\n    Stable: equal elements (neither bef"
    "ore the other) keep their order. Each element moves once.\n    `less` must be a strict weak order (ne"
    "ver `less(a, a)`, transitive); with one that is not, the elements\n    end as a permutation of what t"
    "hey were, in an unspecified order. For a key type `Vec::sort` needs no\n    `less`.\n\n    Example, \"Ve"
    "c::sort_by: stable\":\n        Vec<i32> v = Vec::new();\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 13);\n        Vec::pu"
    "sh(&mut v, 21);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 12);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 25);\n        Vec::sort_by(&"
    "mut v, [](ref<i32, shared> a, ref<i32, shared> b) : bool { *a / 10 < *b / 10 });\n        assert(v[0]"
    " == 13 && v[1] == 12 && v[2] == 21 && v[3] == 25);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::sort\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::so"
    "rt<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v)\u{1f}Sorts `v` in its elements' own order, keeping equal elements in thei"
    "r order.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Vec::sort<T>(ref<Vec<T>, exclusive> v)\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Sorts `v` "
    "in its elements' own order, keeping equal elements in their order.\n\n    `T` must be a key type: inte"
    "gers by value, `false` before `true`, `str` and `String` by their bytes (a\n    prefix first), a stru"
    "ct field by field, an enum by the order its variants are declared in, then by\n    payload. Any other"
    " `T` is rejected at compile time; use `Vec::sort_by` for those.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::binary_searc"
    "h_by\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::binary_search_by<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, ref<T, shared> key, fn(ref<T, shar"
    "ed>, ref<T, shared>) : bool less) : Result<usize, usize>\u{1f}Searches `v`, sorted by `less`, for `key`.\u{1f}"
    "\u{1f}export fn Vec::binary_search_by<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, ref<T, shared> key, fn(ref<T, shared>, re"
    "f<T, shared>) : bool less) : Result<usize, usize>\n    in `std::collections`\n\n    Searches `v`, sorte"
    "d by `less`, for `key`.\n\n    Returns `Ok(i)` with `i` the first position whose element is equal to `"
    "key` (neither goes before the\n    other), or `Err(i)` with `i` the position where `key` would be ins"
    "erted to keep `v` sorted. `v` must be\n    sorted by the same `less`, or the result is unspecified.\n\u{1f}"
    "\u{1e}std::collections\u{1f}Vec::binary_search\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Vec::binary_search<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, probe"
    "<T> key) : Result<usize, usize>\u{1f}Searches `v`, sorted in its elements' own order, for `key`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export "
    "fn Vec::binary_search<T>(ref<Vec<T>, shared> v, probe<T> key) : Result<usize, usize>\n    in `std::co"
    "llections`\n\n    Searches `v`, sorted in its elements' own order, for `key`.\n\n    `T` a key type, as "
    "for `Vec::sort`. `Ok(i)` for the first position holding an element equal to `key`,\n    otherwise `Er"
    "r(i)`, the position where `key` would go.\n\n    Example, \"Vec::binary_search: found and missing\":\n   "
    "     Vec<i32> v = Vec::new();\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 10);\n        Vec::push(&mut v, 20);\n        "
