    "ines of `v`: the parts between `\\n` bytes, each without a `\\r` just before its `\\n`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Stri"
    "ngView::lines(StringView v) : Vec<StringView>\n    in `std::text`\n\n    The lines of `v`: the parts be"
    "tween `\\n` bytes, each without a `\\r` just before its `\\n`.\n\n    There is no empty line after a fina"
    "l `\\n`, so `\"a\\nb\\n\"` and `\"a\\nb\"` both give `a` and `b`; `\"\\n\\n\"`\n    gives two empty lines, and em"
    "pty text none. A `\\r` not followed by `\\n` stays in its line.\n\n    Example, \"StringView::lines: line"
    " endings\":\n        StringView text = \"one\\r\\ntwo\\n\\nthree\\n\";\n        Vec<StringView> ls = StringVie"
    "w::lines(text);\n        assert(Vec::len(&ls) == 4);\n        assert(ls[0] == \"one\");\n        assert(l"
    "s[2] == \"\");\n        assert(ls[3] == \"three\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::text\u{1f}StringView::split_whitespace\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn"
    " StringView::split_whitespace(StringView v) : Vec<StringView>\u{1f}The words of `v`: the runs of bytes th"
    "at are not ASCII white space, in order.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn StringView::split_whitespace(StringView v) : Vec<"
    "StringView>\n    in `std::text`\n\n    The words of `v`: the runs of bytes that are not ASCII white spa"
    "ce, in order.\n\n    No word is empty, so leading, trailing and repeated white space make no differenc"
    "e. White space is what\n    `ascii_is_space` accepts.\n\n    Example, \"StringView::split_whitespace: ru"
    "ns of spaces\":\n        Vec<StringView> w = StringView::split_whitespace(\"  to  be\\tor \");\n        as"
    "sert(Vec::len(&w) == 3);\n        assert(w[0] == \"to\");\n        assert(w[2] == \"or\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::text\u{1f}Str"
    "ingView::code_point\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn StringView::code_point(StringView v) : Option<u32>\u{1f}The code point o"
    "f the first character of `v`, or `None` when `v` is empty.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn StringView::code_point(StringV"
    "iew v) : Option<u32>\n    in `std::text`\n\n    The code point of the first character of `v`, or `None`"
    " when `v` is empty.\n\n    With `StringView::chars`, it reads every character of a text in turn.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std"
    "::text\u{1f}String::push_code_point\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn String::push_code_point(ref<String, exclusive> s, u32 cp"
    ")\u{1f}Appends the character with code point `cp` to `s`, as one to four UTF-8 bytes.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn String::"
    "push_code_point(ref<String, exclusive> s, u32 cp)\n    in `std::text`\n\n    Appends the character with"
    " code point `cp` to `s`, as one to four UTF-8 bytes.\n\n    A value that is no character (a surrogate,"
    " `0xD800` to `0xDFFF`, or above `0x10FFFF`) appends U+FFFD, the\n    replacement character, so `s` st"
    "ays UTF-8.\n\n    Example, \"String::push_code_point: a surrogate\":\n        String s = String::new();\n "
    "       String::push_code_point(&mut s, 0x41);\n        String::push_code_point(&mut s, 0xD800);\n     "
    "   assert(s == \"A\\u{fffd}\");\n\n    Example, \"StringView::lines: a foreach takes the parts as it goes\""
    ":\n        StringView text = \"one\\r\\ntwo\\n\\nthree\\n\";\n        Vec<StringView> ls = StringView::lines("
    "text);\n        usize n = 0;\n        foreach (i, l in StringView::lines(text))\n        {\n            "
    "assert(l == ls[i]);\n            n += 1;\n        }\n        assert(n == 4);\n        n = 0;\n        for"
    "each (w in StringView::split_whitespace(\"  to  be\\tor \"))\n        {\n            n += StringView::len"
    "(w);\n        }\n        assert(n == 6);\n        n = 0;\n        foreach (f in StringView::split(\"a,,b\""
    ", \",\"))\n        {\n            n += 1;\n        }\n        assert(n == 3);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::text\u{1f}StringView::pars"
    "e\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn StringView::parse<T>(StringView v) : Result<T, ParseError>\u{1f}The number the text of `v`"
    " holds, as a `T`: an integer type, `f32` or `f64`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn StringView::parse<T>(StringView v) : R"
    "esult<T, ParseError>\n    in `std::text`\n\n    The number the text of `v` holds, as a `T`: an integer "
    "type, `f32` or `f64`.\n\n    Parsed in place, by the rules and with the errors of `[Parse]` (spec/21 §"
    "2d): the whole text or nothing, so white\n    space around the number must be trimmed first.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::t"
    "ext\u{1f}StringView::parse_radix\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn StringView::parse_radix<T: integer>(StringView v, u32 radix"
    ") : Result<T, ParseError>\u{1f}The integer the text of `v` writes in base `radix` (2 to 36), as a `T`.\u{1f}\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn StringView::parse_radix<T: integer>(StringView v, u32 radix) : Result<T, ParseError>\n    in"
    " `std::text`\n    requires\n        radix >= 2 && radix <= 36, assert_failed, \"StringView::parse_radix"
    ": the radix is from 2 to 36, not %v\", radix\n\n    The integer the text of `v` writes in base `radix` "
    "(2 to 36), as a `T`.\n\n    Digits are `0`-`9` then `a`-`z` or `A`-`Z`, with no prefix such as `0x`; a"
    " leading `-` for a signed `T`.\n    The whole text or nothing, as `StringView::parse`: `Err(Empty)`, "
    "`Err(Invalid(position))` for a character\n    that is no digit of the base, `Err(OutOfRange)` when th"
    "e number does not fit. A radix outside 2 to 36 is\n    a fault.\n\n    Example, \"StringView::parse_radi"
    "x: bases, signs and errors\":\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<u8>(\"ff\", 16) == Ok(255));\n     "
    "   assert(StringView::parse_radix<u8>(\"FF\", 16) == Ok(255));\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<"
    "u8>(\"100\", 16) == Err(OutOfRange));\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<i8>(\"-80\", 16) == Ok(-128"
    "));\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<i8>(\"80\", 16) == Err(OutOfRange));\n        assert(StringV"
    "iew::parse_radix<u32>(\"777\", 8) == Ok(511));\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<u64>(\"1010\", 2) "
    "== Ok(10));\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<u32>(\"zz\", 36) == Ok(1295));\n        assert(Strin"
    "gView::parse_radix<u32>(\"12g\", 16) == Err(Invalid(2)));\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<u32>("
    "\"-1\", 16) == Err(Invalid(0)));\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<i32>(\"-\", 10) == Err(Invalid(1"
    ")));\n        assert(StringView::parse_radix<u32>(\"\", 10) == Err(Empty));\n\n    Example, \"StringView::"
    "parse: no white space\":\n        StringView v = \" 42 \";\n        assert(StringView::parse<u32>(v) == E"
    "rr(Invalid(0)));\n        assert(StringView::parse<u32>(StringView::trim(v)) == Ok(42));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::text\u{1f}"
    "String::append\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn String::append<T>(ref<String, exclusive> s, T x)\u{1f}Appends `x` to `s` as t"
    "ext: the bytes `printf(\"%v\", x)` would write.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn String::append<T>(ref<String, exclusive> s,"
    " T x)\n    in `std::text`\n\n    Appends `x` to `s` as text: the bytes `printf(\"%v\", x)` would write.\n\n"
    "    `x` is a `str`, a `StringView`, a `ref<String, shared>`, an integer, `f32`, `f64` or `bool`; ano"
    "ther type\n    is rejected at the call. A float is written in the shortest digits that read back as t"
    "he same value\n    (`1.0`, `0.1`). `String::appendf` appends formatted text.\n\n    Example, \"String::a"
    "ppend: numbers as printf writes them\":\n        String s = \"x = \";\n        String::append(&mut s, 1.0"
    ");\n        String::append(&mut s, \", n = \");\n        String::append(&mut s, 42: u8);\n        assert("
    "s == \"x = 1.0, n = 42\");\n\n    Example, \"StringView::parse: the three errors\":\n        String a = \"12"
    "x4\";\n        assert(StringView::parse<i32>(&a) == Err(Invalid(2)));\n        String b = \"300\";\n      "
    "  assert(StringView::parse<u8>(&b) == Err(OutOfRange));\n        String c = \"\";\n        assert(String"
    "View::parse<i32>(&c) == Err(Empty));\n        String d = \"-17\";\n        assert(StringView::parse<i32>"
    "(&d) == Ok(-17));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::text\u{1f}String::from_view\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn String::from_view\u{1f}\u{1f}removed\u{1f}export fn St"
