    "4`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn minimum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n  "
    "  in `std::stats`\n\n    The smallest of `data`'s values as an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    "
    "A NaN is passed over: the result is a NaN only when every value is one.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}maximum\u{1f}fn\u{1f}expo"
    "rt fn maximum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The largest of `data`'s values as an `"
    "f64`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn maximum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n"
    "    in `std::stats`\n\n    The largest of `data`'s values as an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n   "
    " A NaN is passed over: the result is a NaN only when every value is one.\n\n    Example, \"minimum and "
    "maximum: as f64, a NaN passed over\":\n        array<i16, 4> a = [7, -3, 12, 0];\n        assert(Option"
    "::unwrap(minimum(&a)) == -3.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(maximum(&a)) == 12.0);\n        f64 nan"
    " = 0.0 / 0.0;\n        array<f64, 3> f = [nan, 2.5, 1.5];\n        assert(Option::unwrap(minimum(&f)) "
    "== 1.5);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(maximum(&f)) == 2.5);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&minimum"
    "(&f[0..0])));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}Summary\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct Summary\u{1f}What `describe` gives: the count of "
    "the values and their minimum, maximum, mean, population standard deviation and median.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struc"
    "t Summary\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    What `describe` gives: the count of the values and their minimum,"
    " maximum, mean, population standard\n    deviation and median.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}describe\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn des"
    "cribe<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<Summary>\u{1f}The count, `minimum`, `maximum`, `mean`, `"
    "stddev` and `median` of `data`'s values as one `Summary`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn desc"
    "ribe<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<Summary>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The count, `minimu"
    "m`, `maximum`, `mean`, `stddev` and `median` of `data`'s values as one `Summary`;\n    `None` for an "
    "empty slice.\n\n    Example, \"describe: a run of timings\":\n        array<u32, 6> ms = [12, 15, 11, 40,"
    " 13, 14];\n        Summary s = Option::unwrap(describe(&ms));\n        assert(s.count == 6 && s.minimu"
    "m == 11.0 && s.maximum == 40.0);\n        assert(s.mean == 17.5 && s.median == 13.5);\n        assert("
    "s.stddev == sqrt(Option::unwrap(variance(&ms))));\n        assert(Option::is_none(&describe(&ms[0..0]"
    ")));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct DateTime\u{1f}A moment in UTC: a date of the proleptic Gre"
    "gorian calendar and a time of day to the second.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    A m"
    "oment in UTC: a date of the proleptic Gregorian calendar and a time of day to the second.\n\n    `year"
    "` 0 is 1 BC and -1 is 2 BC; there are no leap seconds, so every day has 86,400 seconds. Any field\n  "
    "  values can be written in a struct literal: `DateTime::new` checks them, and `DateTime::to_unix` fa"
    "ults\n    on a value that names no moment. Keep moments in UTC and convert with `DateTime::to_local` "
    "only for\n    display.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime: a negative year\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime { .ye"
    "ar = -1, .month = 3, .day = 1, .hour = 9, .minute = 5, .second = 0 };\n        String s = DateTime::t"
    "o_iso(&t);\n        assert(s == \"-0001-03-01T09:05:00Z\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}Weekday\u{1f}enum\u{1f}export enum Weekda"
    "y\u{1f}A day of the week, `Monday` to `Sunday`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export enum Weekday\n    in `std::time`\n\n    A day of the"
    " week, `Monday` to `Sunday`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::from_unix\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_unix(i64 s)"
    " : DateTime\u{1f}The moment `s` seconds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, or before it when `s` is negative.\u{1f}\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn DateTime::from_unix(i64 s) : DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment `s` seconds after "
    "1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, or before it when `s` is negative.\n\n    Every `i64` names a moment, so this ne"
    "ver fails.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::from_unix: before 1970\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime::from_uni"
    "x(-1);\n        assert(t.year == 1969 && t.month == 12 && t.day == 31);\n        assert(t.hour == 23 &"
    "& t.minute == 59 && t.second == 59);\n        assert(DateTime::to_unix(&t) == -1);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTi"
    "me::now\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::now() : DateTime\u{1f}The moment now by the wall clock, to the second: `Da"
    "teTime::from_unix(unix_seconds())`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::now() : DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Th"
    "e moment now by the wall clock, to the second: `DateTime::from_unix(unix_seconds())`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}Da"
    "teTime::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::new(i64 year, u8 month, u8 day, u8 hour, u8 minute, u8 second) : "
    "Option<DateTime>\u{1f}The `DateTime` with these fields, or `None` when they name no moment.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Da"
    "teTime::new(i64 year, u8 month, u8 day, u8 hour, u8 minute, u8 second) : Option<DateTime>\n    in `st"
    "d::time`\n\n    The `DateTime` with these fields, or `None` when they name no moment.\n\n    `None` for "
    "a month 13, a 30 February, a 29 February in a common year, an hour 24 or a second 60. A leap\n    yea"
    "r is divisible by 4 and not by 100, or by 400.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::new: 29 February\":\n        O"
    "ption<DateTime> leap = DateTime::new(2024, 2, 29, 0, 0, 0);\n        Option<DateTime> common = DateTi"
    "me::new(2023, 2, 29, 0, 0, 0);\n        assert(Option::is_some(&leap));\n        assert(Option::is_non"
    "e(&common));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::to_unix\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_unix(ref<DateTime, shared> t) :"
    " i64\u{1f}The seconds from 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z to the moment `t`, negative before it; the inverse of `fr"
    "om_unix`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_unix(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : i64\n    in `std::time`\n    require"
    "s\n        DateTime::valid(t), invalid_datetime\n\n    The seconds from 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z to the mom"
    "ent `t`, negative before it; the inverse of `from_unix`.\n\n    Faults (`invalid_datetime`) when the f"
    "ields of `t` name no moment, or when the count does not fit in an\n    `i64`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::w"
    "eekday\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::weekday(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : Weekday\u{1f}The day of the week of the "
    "date of `t`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::weekday(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : Weekday\n    in `std::time`\n\n   "
    " The day of the week of the date of `t`.\n\n    Defined for every valid `t`, however far from 1970. Fa"
    "ults (`invalid_datetime`) when the date of `t`\n    names no day.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::weekday: 1"
    "970-01-01\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime::from_unix(0);\n        Weekday w = DateTime::weekday(&t);\n"
    "        assert(Weekday::text(&w) == \"Thursday\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}Weekday::text\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Weekday::tex"
    "t(ref<Weekday, shared> w) : str\u{1f}The day's English name (`Monday`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Weekday::text(ref<Week"
    "day, shared> w) : str\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The day's English name (`Monday`).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTim"
    "e::to_iso\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_iso(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\u{1f}The moment in ISO 8601 for"
    "m in UTC, such as `2026-10-03T10:17:00Z`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_iso(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : Str"
    "ing\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment in ISO 8601 form in UTC, such as `2026-10-03T10:17:00Z`.\n\n   "
    " Each field is zero-padded to its width. A year outside 0 ..= 9999 is written with its sign and at l"
    "east\n    four digits (`-0001`, `+10000`). Use `iso_from_unix_ms` for a timestamp with milliseconds.\n"
    "\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::from_iso\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_iso(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, P"
    "arseError>\u{1f}Reads a moment in `to_iso`'s form, or a date alone (`YYYY-MM-DD`) as its midnight.\u{1f}\u{1f}expor"
    "t fn DateTime::from_iso(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Read"
    "s a moment in `to_iso`'s form, or a date alone (`YYYY-MM-DD`) as its midnight.\n\n    The year is four"
    " digits, or a sign and four or more. The seconds may carry a fraction (`:37.25Z`), which\n    is drop"
    "ped; `unix_ms_from_iso` keeps it to the millisecond. No other form is read.\n\n    - `Err(Empty)` for "
    "the empty text.\n    - `Err(Invalid(i))`: byte `i` is the first that does not fit the form (the lengt"
    "h when the text stops\n      short).\n    - `Err(OutOfRange)`: the fields name no moment (a month 13, "
    "a 29 February in a common year, a second\n      60), or the year is beyond `i64`.\n\n    Example, \"Date"
    "Time::from_iso: a date alone, a short time\":\n        match (DateTime::from_iso(\"2026-10-03\"))\n      "
    "  {\n            Ok(t)  : assert(t.day == 3 && t.hour == 0),\n            Err(_) : assert(false),\n    "
    "    }\n        match (DateTime::from_iso(\"2026-10-03T10:17\"))\n        {\n            Err(Invalid(i)) :"
    " assert(i == 16),\n            _               : assert(false),\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::eq\u{1f}fn"
    "\u{1f}export fn DateTime::eq(ref<DateTime, shared> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\u{1f}Whether `a` and `b`"
    " are the same moment, every field equal.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::eq(ref<DateTime, shared> a, ref<DateTi"
    "me, shared> b) : bool\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Whether `a` and `b` are the same moment, every field e"
    "qual.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::less\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::less(ref<DateTime, shared> a, ref<DateTime, "
    "shared> b) : bool\u{1f}Whether `a` is earlier than `b`, comparing the fields in order of significance.\u{1f}\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn DateTime::less(ref<DateTime, shared> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\n    in `std::time`\n"
    "\n    Whether `a` is earlier than `b`, comparing the fields in order of significance.\n\n    For valid "
    "values this is the order of `to_unix`. It fits `Vec::sort_by`.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::less: orderi"
    "ng\":\n        DateTime a = DateTime::from_unix(100);\n        DateTime b = DateTime::from_unix(160);\n "
    "       assert(DateTime::less(&a, &b));\n        assert(!DateTime::less(&b, &a));\n        assert(!Date"
    "Time::eq(&a, &b));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}unix_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_ms() : i64\u{1f}The wall clock in milliseconds s"
    "ince 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, rounded down.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_ms() : i64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The wall"
    " clock in milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, rounded down.\n\n    `unix_ms() / 1000` is `unix_se"
    "conds()` when read together. The wall clock may be changed while the\n    program runs; measure elaps"
    "ed time with `monotonic_ns`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}local_offset_seconds\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn local_offset_seconds(i64 "
    "unix) : i64\u{1f}How many seconds the machine's local time is ahead of UTC at the moment `unix` seconds a"
    "fter 1970.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn local_offset_seconds(i64 unix) : i64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    How many seconds "
    "the machine's local time is ahead of UTC at the moment `unix` seconds after 1970.\n\n    Negative when"
    " local time is behind UTC; daylight saving included, by the operating system's rule for the\n    zone"
    " the program runs in. Between -64800 and 64800. For a moment the system has no rule for, the offset\n"
    "    now. It differs between machines, and on one machine between moments.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::to_l"
    "ocal\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_local(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : DateTime\u{1f}The moment `t`, given in UT"
    "C, written in the machine's local time.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_local(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : Dat"
    "eTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment `t`, given in UTC, written in the machine's local time.\n\n  "
    "  The result is a `DateTime` like any other and knows nothing of its zone: print its fields, not `to"
    "_iso`\n    (which writes `Z`), and keep the UTC value for arithmetic, since `to_unix` of the result i"
    "s the moment\n    plus the offset. Faults (`invalid_datetime`) when `t` names no moment, or when the "
    "moment is within a\n    day of an `i64` limit.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}monotonic_ns\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn monotonic_ns() :"
    " u64\u{1f}Nanoseconds on a monotonic clock, from a start fixed for the whole program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn monotoni"
    "c_ns() : u64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Nanoseconds on a monotonic clock, from a start fixed for the wh"
    "ole program.\n\n    The clock never goes backwards, so the difference of two readings measures how lon"
    "g something took. Its\n    values mean nothing else; use `unix_ms` for the time of day.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}u"
    "nix_seconds\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_seconds() : i64\u{1f}The wall clock: whole seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:0"
    "0Z.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_seconds() : i64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The wall clock: whole seconds since 1970"
    "-01-01T00:00:00Z.\n\n    The wall clock may be changed while the program runs; measure elapsed time wi"
    "th `monotonic_ns`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}sleep_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn sleep_ms(u64 ms)\u{1f}Makes the calling thread do no"
    "thing for at least `ms` milliseconds; other threads run meanwhile.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sleep_ms(u64 ms)\n    i"
    "n `std::time`\n\n    Makes the calling thread do nothing for at least `ms` milliseconds; other threads"
    " run meanwhile.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}iso_from_unix_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn iso_from_unix_ms(i64 ms) : String\u{1f}The mome"
    "nt `ms` milliseconds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z in `to_iso`'s form, with milliseconds.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn is"
    "o_from_unix_ms(i64 ms) : String\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment `ms` milliseconds after 1970-01-0"
    "1T00:00:00Z in `to_iso`'s form, with milliseconds.\n\n    The form is `2026-10-07T12:34:56.789Z`, a lo"
    "g line's or a JSON document's timestamp. A negative `ms`\n    is before 1970, the milliseconds still "
    "counted forward within their second.\n\n    Example, \"iso_from_unix_ms: before 1970\":\n        assert(i"
    "so_from_unix_ms(-1) == \"1969-12-31T23:59:59.999Z\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}unix_ms_from_iso\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_m"
    "s_from_iso(StringView text) : Result<i64, ParseError>\u{1f}The milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z of"
    " a moment in `DateTime::from_iso`'s form.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_ms_from_iso(StringView text) : Result<i64,"
    " ParseError>\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z of a moment in `Dat"
    "eTime::from_iso`'s form.\n\n    A fraction of the seconds is kept to the millisecond: its first three "
    "digits, fewer filled with zeros,\n    further digits ignored (`.5` is 500, `.1234` is 123). The error"
    "s are `from_iso`'s; `Err(OutOfRange)`\n    also when the milliseconds do not fit in an `i64`.\n\n    Ex"
    "ample, \"unix_ms_from_iso: fractions\":\n        assert(unix_ms_from_iso(\"1970-01-01T00:00:01.5Z\") == O"
    "k(1500));\n        assert(unix_ms_from_iso(\"1970-01-01T00:00:00.1239Z\") == Ok(123));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}Date"
    "Time::to_http_date\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_http_date(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\u{1f}The moment "
    "in HTTP's date form, such as `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_http_date(ref<"
    "DateTime, shared> t) : String\n    in `std::time`\n    requires\n        t.year >= 0 && t.year <= 9999,"
    " invalid_datetime\n\n    The moment in HTTP's date form, such as `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT`.\n\n   "
    " The form (RFC 9110 §5.6.7, the IMF-fixdate) is what the `Date`, `Last-Modified` and `Expires` head"
    "ers\n    carry. Faults (`invalid_datetime`) for a year outside 0 ..= 9999, or fields that name no mom"
    "ent.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::to_http_date: the IMF-fixdate\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime::from_un"
    "ix(784111777);\n        assert(DateTime::to_http_date(&t) == \"Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std:"
    ":time\u{1f}DateTime::from_http_date\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_http_date(StringView text) : Result<DateT"
    "ime, ParseError>\u{1f}Reads an HTTP date in any of the three forms a recipient must accept (RFC 9110 §5."
    "6.7).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_http_date(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\n    in `st"
    "d::time`\n\n    Reads an HTTP date in any of the three forms a recipient must accept (RFC 9110 §5.6.7"
    ").\n\n    The forms are `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT` (the one to send), the obsolete RFC 850\n    `S"
    "unday, 06-Nov-94 08:49:37 GMT` (a two-digit year: 70 to 99 are 1970 to 1999, 00 to 69 are 2000 to\n  "
    "  2069) and asctime's `Sun Nov  6 08:49:37 1994`. The words are separated by ASCII whitespace; the w"
    "eekday\n    is not checked against the date.\n\n    - `Err(Empty)` when the text has no words.\n    - `E"
    "rr(Invalid(i))`: `i` is the start of the first word that does not fit.\n    - `Err(OutOfRange)`: the "
    "fields name no moment.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::from_http_date: a two-digit year\":\n        match (Da"
    "teTime::from_http_date(\"Sunday, 06-Nov-94 08:49:37 GMT\"))\n        {\n            Ok(t)  : assert(t.ye"
    "ar == 1994 && t.month == 11 && t.day == 6),\n            Err(_) : assert(false),\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::pro"
    "cess\u{1f}Command\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct Command\u{1f}A program to run: its name or path, its arguments, its wor"
    "king directory and its environment.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct Command\n    in `std::process`\n\n    A program to r"
    "un: its name or path, its arguments, its working directory and its environment.\n\n    Made by `Comman"
    "d::new`, changed by `arg`, `current_dir`, `env` and `clear_env`, and run by `output`,\n    `output_wi"
    "th_input`, `status` or `spawn`, any number of times. No shell is involved: each argument\n    reaches"
    " the program exactly as given, with no splitting, quoting or expansion; a program that wants a\n    s"
    "hell runs one by name (`/bin/sh` with `-c`).\n\n    Running fails with `Err(NotFound)` when there is n"
    "o such program, `Err(Denied)` when it may not be run,\n    and `Err(Io)` for any other failure to sta"
    "rt it (a working directory that does not exist among them).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Output\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export stru"
    "ct Output\u{1f}How a run of `Command::output` ended, and everything the program wrote.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct Out"
    "put\n    in `std::process`\n\n    How a run of `Command::output` ended, and everything the program wrot"
    "e.\n\n    - `status`: the exit code, or on Unix minus the number of the signal that ended it (`-9` for"
    " SIGKILL).\n    - `stdout`: every byte the program wrote to its standard output.\n    - `stderr`: ever"
    "y byte the program wrote to its standard error.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::ne"
    "w(StringView program) : Command\u{1f}A command running `program` with no arguments, in this program's wor"
    "king directory and environment.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::new(StringView program) : Command\n    in `std::p"
    "rocess`\n\n    A command running `program` with no arguments, in this program's working directory and "
    "environment.\n\n    A name without a separator is looked for on `PATH`, as the system does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::pro"
    "cess\u{1f}Command::arg\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::arg(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView a)\u{1f}Appends one arg"
    "ument, passed to the program exactly as given.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::arg(ref<Command, exclusive> c, St"
    "ringView a)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Appends one argument, passed to the program exactly as given."
    "\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::current_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::current_dir(ref<Command, exclusive> c, St"
    "ringView dir)\u{1f}Sets the working directory the program starts in.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::current_dir(ref<"
    "Command, exclusive> c, StringView dir)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Sets the working directory the pro"
    "gram starts in.\n\n    A directory that does not exist makes the run fail with `Err(Io)`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::proce"
    "ss\u{1f}Command::env\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::env(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView name, StringView val"
    "ue)\u{1f}Sets the environment variable `name` to `value` for the program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::env(ref<Com"
    "mand, exclusive> c, StringView name, StringView value)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Sets the environme"
    "nt variable `name` to `value` for the program.\n\n    The variables set this way go on top of the inhe"
    "rited environment, or of an empty one after\n    `Command::clear_env`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::clear_"
    "env\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::clear_env(ref<Command, exclusive> c)\u{1f}Makes the program start with no envir"
    "onment variables but those `Command::env` sets.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::clear_env(ref<Command, exclusive"
    "> c)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Makes the program start with no environment variables but those `Com"
    "mand::env` sets.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::output\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::output(ref<Command, shared> c)"
    " : Result<Output, FileError>\u{1f}Runs the program to its end with an empty standard input, and gives its"
    " status and everything it wrote.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::output(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Output,"
    " FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Runs the program to its end with an empty standard input, and"
    " gives its status and everything it wrote.\n\n    Both streams are read at once, so neither pipe fills"
    " and stops the program.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::output_with_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::output_with_"
    "input(ref<Command, shared> c, slice<u8, shared> input) : Result<Output, FileError>\u{1f}Runs the program "
    "to its end with `input` as its standard input, as `Command::output` does.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::output"
    "_with_input(ref<Command, shared> c, slice<u8, shared> input) : Result<Output, FileError>\n    in `std"
    "::process`\n\n    Runs the program to its end with `input` as its standard input, as `Command::output`"
    " does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::status\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::status(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<"
    "i32, FileError>\u{1f}Runs the program to its end with this program's standard input, output and error, an"
    "d gives its status.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::status(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<i32, FileError>\n    "
    "in `std::process`\n\n    Runs the program to its end with this program's standard input, output and er"
    "ror, and gives its status.\n\n    The status is `Output::status`'s: the exit code, or on Unix minus th"
    "e signal that ended it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Child\u{1f}A running child pro"
    "cess, its standard input, output and error each a pipe to this program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Chil"
    "d\n    in `std::process`\n\n    A running child process, its standard input, output and error each a pi"
    "pe to this program.\n\n    Made by `Command::spawn`. Destroying a `Child` closes its pipes but does no"
    "t end it: a running child\n    runs on. A child that fills a pipe nobody reads stops until it is read"
    ". `Child::take_input` and\n    `Child::take_output` move its pipes out, for use from other threads.\n\u{1f}"
    "\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::spawn\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::spawn(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Child, Fil"
    "eError>\u{1f}Starts the program and returns at once, its three streams pipes to this program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn "
    "Command::spawn(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Child, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Starts "
    "the program and returns at once, its three streams pipes to this program.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::id\u{1f}f"
    "n\u{1f}export fn Child::id(ref<Child, shared> ch) : u64\u{1f}The operating system's number for the child (its "
    "process id).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::id(ref<Child, shared> ch) : u64\n    in `std::process`\n\n    The operat"
    "ing system's number for the child (its process id).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::write_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn C"
    "hild::write_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, slice<u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Writes"
    " all of `data` to the child's standard input.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::write_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch"
    ", slice<u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Writes all of `data` "
    "to the child's standard input.\n\n    `Err(Io)` when the child has ended or closed its input; it never"
    " ends this program. Does nothing once\n    `Child::take_input` has taken the input.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Ch"
    "ild::close_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::close_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch)\u{1f}Closes the child's standa"
    "rd input, so that the child reads its end.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::close_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch)\n "
    "   in `std::process`\n\n    Closes the child's standard input, so that the child reads its end.\n\n    D"
    "oes nothing once `Child::take_input` has taken the input.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_output\u{1f}fn\u{1f}expor"
    "t fn Child::read_output(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<u"
    "size, FileError>\u{1f}Appends to `buf` what has arrived on the child's standard output, up to `max` bytes"
    ", and returns how many.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::read_output(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusi"
    "ve> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Appends to `buf` what has "
    "arrived on the child's standard output, up to `max` bytes, and returns how many.\n\n    Waits for at l"
    "east one byte; at most a MiB is read at a time. `Ok(0)` (for a `max` above 0) means the\n    end of t"
    "he output.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_output_line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_output_line(ref<Child, ex"
    "clusive> ch) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The next line of the child's standard output, witho"
    "ut its `'\\n'` or a `'\\r'` just before it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::read_output_line(ref<Child, exclusive> c"
    "h) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    The next line of the child's stand"
    "ard output, without its `'\\n'` or a `'\\r'` just before it.\n\n    `Ok(None)` at the end of the output;"
    " `Err(Utf8(e))` for a line that is not UTF-8.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_error\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::r"
    "ead_error(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileErro"
    "r>\u{1f}Appends to `buf` what has arrived on the child's standard error, as `Child::read_output` does.\u{1f}\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn Child::read_error(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Resul"
    "t<usize, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Appends to `buf` what has arrived on the child's stan"
    "dard error, as `Child::read_output` does.\n\n    What `Child::read_error_line` has read ahead comes fi"
    "rst.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_error_line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_error_line(ref<Child, exclusive>"
    " ch) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The next line of the child's standard error, as `Child::rea"
    "d_output_line` reads standard output.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::read_error_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : "
